.Dd April 22, 2026
.Dt AS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm as
.Nd Substrate assembler frontend
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 32 | Fl 64 | Fl -32 | Fl -64
.Op Fl c
.Op Fl g
.Op Fl I Ar dir
.Op Fl D Ar macro Ns Op = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl march Ar cpu
.Op Fl mtune Ar cpu
.Op Fl mx86-used-note Ns = Ns Cm no
.Op Fl O Ar elf|binary
.Op Fl msyntax Ns = Ns Ar att|intel
.Op Fl -warn | Fl W | Fl -no-warn
.Op Fl -fatal-warnings
.Op Fl al Ns Op = Ns Ar file
.Op Fl -defsym Ar sym=val
.Op Fl -statistics
.Op Fl -target Ns = Ns Ar name
.Op Fl -help
.Op Fl -target-help
.Op Fl -version
.Op Fl Wa Ar opts
.Op Fl -max-input-bytes Ar n
.Op Fl -max-line-bytes Ar n
.Op Fl -max-token-length Ar n
.Op Fl -max-macro-depth Ar n
.Op Fl -max-include-depth Ar n
.Op Fl -max-expansion-bytes Ar n
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the Substrate assembler command-line frontend.
.Pp
Each
.Ar file
is a source, and several are assembled as one, in the order given: a
section chosen in one is still the section in the next, and a label of
one may be used in another.
A
.Ar file
of
.Sq -
is the standard input, which is also what is assembled when no
.Ar file
is named and the standard input is not a terminal.
A file whose name ends in
.Pa .S
is first given to the C preprocessor, if it is the only one.
.Pp
It provides GNU-compatible assembler option handling, source preflight limits,
native in-tree parsing/encoding, and ELF post-validation through
Li elfobj(3) .
.Pp
Current behavior is standalone:
.Nm
lexes, parses, encodes, relaxes, and emits output directly in-tree.
No external assembler handoff is performed.
.Pp
Alias binaries
.Nm as.x86
and
.Nm as.x64
select 32-bit and 64-bit defaults respectively.
.Pp
When installed through the host toolchain workflow described in
.Xr host_toolchain 7 ,
the installed
.Xr cc 1
driver resolves the sibling assembler from the same prefix before falling back
to other tools on
.Ev PATH .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--max-token-length"
.It Fl 32 , Fl -32
Select 32-bit mode (i386 output contract).
.It Fl 64 , Fl -64
Select 64-bit mode (x86-64 output contract).
.It Fl o Ar output
Write the object to
.Ar output .
Without it the object is written to
.Pa a.out
in the current directory.
The file is a new one, with what the process's mask leaves of read and
write for all.
.Ar output
may be a device or a pipe, or a link: a link to a file that is not there
yet has the file made, and one to a file that is there is replaced by
the object.
It may not be one of the sources, by any name.
.It Fl c
Accepted for compatibility; output remains an object file.
.It Fl g , Fl -gen-debug , Fl -gdwarf- Ns Ar N
For a source that has no
.Li .loc
of its own, make a line table of the source itself, each instruction a
row of its line, and a compile unit in
.Li .debug_info
that names the source and the table.
A source with
.Li .loc
has the table those say, with the option or without it; and what a
compiler writes in
.Li .debug_*
sections is assembled as it is written.
The table is of DWARF 5 if the source has a
.Li .file 0 ,
and of DWARF 3 if not, whatever
.Ar N
is.
.It Fl I Ar dir
Add
.Ar dir
to the directories searched for the file of an
.Ic .include .
The name is tried as written first, from the current directory, and
then under each directory in the order given.
A name that begins with a slash is not searched for.
An included file is expanded in its place: its macros are defined for
what follows it, and the macros and conditionals of the file that
includes it apply in it.
.It Fl D Ar macro Ns Op = Ns Ar value
Define a name for the preprocessor.
Only a source whose name ends in
.Pa .S
is put through the preprocessor; for any other the option has no effect.
.It Fl march Ar cpu Ns Op + Ns Ar extension ...
Name the processor the code is for.
The names are those of GNU as:
.Cm generic32 , generic64 , i386 , i486 , i586 , pentium , i686 ,
.Cm pentiumpro , pentiumii , pentiumiii , pentium4 , prescott , nocona ,
.Cm core , core2 , corei7 , k6 , k6_2 , athlon , opteron , k8 , amdfam10 ,
.Cm bdver1
to
.Cm bdver4 ,
.Cm znver1
to
.Cm znver6 ,
.Cm btver1
and
.Cm btver2 ;
each may be followed by extensions of GNU as's naming, such as
.Cm +avx2
or
.Cm +nosse4a .
A name that is none of them is refused, and so is a processor that has
no 64-bit mode when that is the mode.
This assembler also takes
.Cm x86-64 ,
.Cm x86-64-v1
to
.Cm x86-64-v4 ,
.Cm amd64 ,
.Cm generic
and
.Cm native ,
which GNU as does not; the levels are 64-bit only, and are recorded in
the object's
.Li .note.gnu.property .
The processor does not limit the instructions that are assembled: every
instruction this assembler has is taken whatever is named.
.It Fl mtune Ar cpu
Select tuning target string.
.It Fl mx86-used-note Ns = Ns Cm no
Taken, and changes nothing: the note of the instruction sets a source
used
.Pq Dq x86 ISA used ,
which some builds of GNU as put in
.Li .note.gnu.property
unasked, is not written.
.Cm yes
is refused.
.It Fl O Ar elf|binary
Select output format.
.Cm elf
is the default and emits ET_REL objects.
.Cm binary
emits flat raw bytes with section-layout policy
.Pq .text , .rodata , .data , then .bss .
Binary mode rejects unresolved relocations and ELF-only metadata directives.
The image begins at address 0: a label written as data
.Pq Li ".long label"
is its offset from the start of the image, in whichever section it is,
and a symbol the source does not define is an error.
.It Fl msyntax Ns = Ns Ar att|intel
Select the x86 syntax the source begins in.
An
.Ic .intel_syntax
or
.Ic .att_syntax
directive in the source changes it from that line on.
.It Fl -warn
Write warnings to the standard error, each once.
This is the default.
.It Fl W , Fl -no-warn
Write no warnings.
.It Fl -fatal-warnings
If a warning is written, fail after it: the exit status is 1 and no
output file is left.
A warning that
.Fl -no-warn
has silenced does not count.
.It Fl al Ns Op = Ns Ar file
Write a listing: each line of the source with its number and, where it
put bytes in the object, the offset in its section at which they begin
and the bytes, four to a line, on at most four lines after the first.
The layout of a line is GNU as's:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
   2 0000 B8010000 	f:	mov $1, %eax
   2      00
   3 0005 C3       		ret
.Ed
.Pp
What a macro makes is listed on the line that calls it, and what a
repeat makes on the lines of its body.
Lines of an included file are not listed, and there are no tables of
symbols.
If
.Ar file
is omitted, listing path defaults to
.Ar output
with
.Pa .lst
suffix; GNU as writes to the standard output.
.It Fl -defsym Ar sym=val
Define
.Ar sym
as an absolute symbol with the value
.Ar val ,
a number or an expression of numbers, before the first line of the source.
It may be used wherever a symbol made by
.Ic .set
may, and
.Ic .if ,
.Ic .ifdef
and
.Ic .ifndef
see it.
With no value it is 0.
An option that is not of this form is an error.
.It Fl -statistics
Print wall-clock/resource usage statistics.
.It Fl -help
List the options, on the standard output, and exit.
Every option that is taken is in the list.
.It Fl mthumb , Fl marm
For ARM: the instruction set the source begins in.
Refused when the machine is not ARM.
.It Fl mcpu Ns = Ns Ar name , Fl mfpu Ns = Ns Ar name , Fl mfloat-abi Ns = Ns Ar name
For ARM and AArch64: taken, and nothing changed by them, every
instruction
.Nm
has for the machine being allowed.
Refused when the machine is x86.
.It Fl -version , Fl v
Print what this assembler is, and exit.
It names itself the Substrate assembler: it is not GNU
.Nm ,
and a script that asks should not be told it is.
.It Fl -target Ns = Ns Ar name
The machine to assemble for:
.Cm i386 ,
.Cm x86_64 ,
.Cm arm
or
.Cm aarch64 .
Without it the machine is x86, or the one of the name
.Nm
is run as.
See
.Sx ARCHITECTURE SYNTAX .
.It Fl -target-help
Print target summary and exit.
.It Fl Wa Ar opts
The options in
.Ar opts ,
which are parted by commas, as if each had been given by itself:
.Fl Wa,--defsym,X=1
is
.Fl -defsym Cm X=1 .
A compiler's driver passes its own
.Fl Wa
through, so this is what such an option arrives as.
.It Fl -noexecstack , Fl -from-cc
Accepted, with no effect: compilers' drivers pass them,
.Xr cc 1
the last.
.It Fl -max-input-bytes Ar n
Refuse a source of more than
.Ar n
bytes, as it is read and before anything in it is expanded.
.It Fl -max-line-bytes Ar n
Refuse a source with a line of more than
.Ar n
bytes, not counting the newline that ends it.
.It Fl -max-token-length Ar n
Refuse a source in which more than
.Ar n
bytes stand together with no white space between them.
.It Fl -max-macro-depth Ar n
Refuse a source whose macros are expanded more than
.Ar n
deep, one calling the next; 64 if not given.
It is what stops a macro that calls itself without end.
.It Fl -max-include-depth Ar n
Refuse a source whose
.Cm .include
directives are nested more than
.Ar n
deep.
.It Fl -max-expansion-bytes Ar n
Refuse a source that comes to more than
.Ar n
bytes once its macros and repeats are expanded; 256 MiB if not given.
.El
.Pp
Each limit is a positive decimal number; zero, a negative number and
anything else are refused.
.Pp
An option that is none of these is an error: the assembler names it,
assembles nothing, and exits with status 1.
.Sh DIRECTIVES
.Nm
tracks GNU-style directive families used by Substrate userland and
toolchain flows, including:
.Bl -tag -width ".cfi_startproc/.cfi_endproc"
.It Data
.Cm .byte , .short/.hword , .long/.int , .quad/.8byte , .float , .double ,
.Cm .ascii , .asciz/.string , .base64 , .zero/.space , .fill , .skip , .org , .incbin ,
.Cm .value , .2byte , .4byte , .single , .tfloat , .octa ,
.Cm .uleb128 , .sleb128 , .string8/.string16/.string32/.string64 ,
.Cm .dc , .dc.b/.w/.l/.a/.s/.d/.x , .ds , .ds.b/.w/.l/.s/.d/.x/.p ,
.Cm .dcb , .dcb.b/.w/.l/.s/.d/.x .
On x86,
.Cm .nops Ar size Ns Op , Ar most
is
.Ar size
bytes of instructions that do nothing, as an alignment is padded with
them: the long ones of the object, none longer than
.Ar most
bytes if that is given and not 0, and a jump over the rest where
there are many.
A floating-point number is a decimal one, which may have
.Li 0
and a letter before it
.Pq Li 0f1.5 , 0d2.5 ;
or
.Li inf , nan , qnan
or
.Li snan ;
or its bits, as
.Li 0x:3fc00000 .
One too large for the width is an error.
In a string
.Li \eb , \ef , \en , \er , \et , \e\e
and
.Li \e\(dq
are what they are in C,
.Li \ex
and up to three octal digits give a byte, and any other character after
a backslash is itself.
An argument of
.Cm .uleb128
or
.Cm .sleb128
may be the distance between two labels.
An argument of
.Cm .byte , .short , .long
or
.Cm .quad
is a number; or a symbol and a number, which is a relocation of the
directive's width; or that less a place in the directive's own section
(a label of it, or
.Ql \&. ) ,
which is a relocation relative to the field.
Two labels of one section differ by a number.
Any other expression is an error.
.It Sections and layout
.Cm .text , .data , .bss , .rodata , .section , .pushsection , .popsection ,
.Cm .previous , .subsection , .group , .align/.balign/.p2align ,
.Cm .balignw/.balignl , .p2alignw/.p2alignl , .struct .
A subsection number, given by
.Cm .subsection ,
.Cm .text Ar n ,
.Cm .data Ar n
or as the second argument of
.Cm .pushsection ,
places what follows in that numbered part of the section; the parts
are assembled in ascending order of number.
.Cm .struct
lays what follows out from an offset and assembles it into nothing: its
labels are absolute symbols.
.Pp
An alignment, a count or a fill may be an expression of symbols set to
numbers; a count may also be the distance between two labels, or name a
symbol set further down.
An alignment of 0, or none, is 1; it must be a power of two, and the
widest is 2 to the power 20.
A section is aligned as far as the widest alignment asked for within it.
.Cm .popsection
brings back both the section
.Cm .pushsection
was written in and the one before it; with nothing pushed it is warned
of and passed over.
A section named a second time must have the flags, type and entry size
it was first given.
.It Messages
.Cm .error , .err
and
.Cm .abort
fail the assembly;
.Cm .warning
warns;
.Cm .print
writes a line to standard output;
.Cm .fail
is an error below 500 and a warning from there.
A directive that is not known is an error.
.It Symbols
.Cm .globl/.global , .local , .weak , .comm , .lcomm , .type , .size ,
.Cm .hidden , .protected , .internal , .symver .
.Cm .lcomm ,
and
.Cm .comm
of a name that a
.Cm .local
above it names, reserve storage in
.Ql .bss
for a local symbol; any other
.Cm .comm
is a common symbol for the linker to place.
.Pp
A symbol set by
.Cm .set ,
.Cm .equ
or
.Ql =
to what is no number is another name for it: another symbol and a
number, the place of the directive
.Pq Ql \&. ,
or the distance between two labels of one section.
It may be set again; a label may take its name; but a name that is a
label may not be set, and
.Cm .equiv
sets a name once, that nothing has set.
Names that come round to themselves are an error.
.Cm .type
takes
.Ql @function ,
.Ql @object ,
.Ql @tls_object ,
.Ql @common ,
.Ql @notype ,
.Ql @gnu_indirect_function
and
.Ql @gnu_unique_object ,
with
.Ql %
for
.Ql @ ,
in quotes, or as
.Ql STT_FUNC
and its fellows; any other name is an error.
The size
.Cm .size
gives is a number or the distance between two labels.
A symbol the source names and does not define is global, whatever
.Cm .local
says of it.
.Cm .symver Ar name , Ar name Ns @ Ns Ar version
makes a symbol of that name beside
.Ar name
.Po
.Ql @@
for the version a plain reference gets,
.Ql @@@
for the only name the symbol is to have
.Pc ,
which the linker reads.
.It Conditionals and macros
.Cm .if/.ifdef/.ifndef/.else/.endif , .macro/.endm , .rept/.endr , .irp/.irpc
.Pp
A condition may also be a number held to zero \(em
.Cm .ifeq , .ifne , .ifge , .ifgt , .ifle , .iflt
\(em two strings held to each other \(em
.Cm .ifc , .ifnc , .ifeqs , .ifnes
\(em or whether an argument is blank,
.Cm .ifb
and
.Cm .ifnb ;
and
.Cm .elseif
is an
.Cm .else
with a condition of its own.
In a macro
.Cm .exitm
ends the expansion, and
.Cm .purgem
forgets a macro.
.It Debug/CFI
.Cm .file , .loc , .cfi_*
.Pp
The
.Cm .cfi_*
directives are made the call frame information of
.Ql .eh_frame ,
or of
.Ql .debug_frame
where
.Cm .cfi_sections
asks for it: a CIE for the procedures that begin alike and an FDE for
each, with a relocation for each address.
.Cm .cfi_startproc , .cfi_endproc , .cfi_def_cfa , .cfi_def_cfa_register ,
.Cm .cfi_def_cfa_offset , .cfi_adjust_cfa_offset , .cfi_offset ,
.Cm .cfi_rel_offset , .cfi_val_offset , .cfi_register , .cfi_restore ,
.Cm .cfi_undefined , .cfi_same_value , .cfi_remember_state ,
.Cm .cfi_restore_state , .cfi_return_column , .cfi_signal_frame ,
.Cm .cfi_escape , .cfi_personality , .cfi_lsda , .cfi_window_save
and
.Cm .cfi_sections
are those there are; any other is refused by its name.
An offset is a number, and a register is one by its name or by its
DWARF number.
.Pp
.Cm .file
and
.Cm .loc
are made the line table of
.Ql .debug_line :
the files by their numbers, and a row for each
.Cm .loc ,
which says a file's number, a line and a column, and then any of
.Cm is_stmt Ar 0|1 , basic_block , prologue_end , epilogue_begin ,
.Cm isa Ar n , discriminator Ar n
and
.Cm view Ar name .
A row is at the instruction that follows its
.Cm .loc ,
or at the next
.Cm .loc
if none comes between; one with a view is where it stands; and one that
nothing follows is no row.
The name of a view is its number \(em how many rows stand before it at
the same address \(em wherever a number is written, and is no symbol;
.Cm view -0
begins the count again.
A file number that
.Cm .file
has given to one file is refused for another.
.It Taken, and nothing done
Directives that say nothing of the bytes are taken so that source
written for GNU
.Nm
assembles, and have no effect:
.Cm .extern
(every symbol not defined is another file's already),
.Cm .ident ,
.Cm .version ,
.Cm .gnu_attribute ,
.Cm .addrsig
and
.Cm .addrsig_sym ,
.Cm .sse_check
and
.Cm .operand_check ,
.Cm .loc_mark_labels ,
.Cm .line
and
.Cm .ln ,
the listing's
.Cm .eject , .list , .nolist , .title , .sbttl
and
.Cm .psize ,
the debugging format of
.Cm .stabs , .stabn
and
.Cm .stabd ,
which is not written, and
.Cm .noaltmacro ,
macros being read that way and
.Cm .altmacro
not taken.
A directive that is none of these and none above is an error.
.El
.Sh ARCHITECTURE SYNTAX
.Bl -tag -width "ARM and AArch64"
.It x86 / x86-64
AT&T syntax is default.
Intel syntax is available with
.Fl msyntax=intel .
In AT&T syntax a register is written with
.Sq % ,
and a name without one is a symbol, whatever it spells:
.Ql call sp
calls the function
.Va sp .
A statement in Intel syntax is assembled as the AT&T statement it is:
its operands in the other order, and the size written on memory,
.Cm byte|word|dword|fword|qword|tbyte|xmmword|ymmword|zmmword ptr ,
for the suffix of the instruction where it has one.
Memory with no size written, in an instruction that has nothing else
to tell its size from, is refused:
.Ql inc [eax] .
What a compiler writes with
.Fl masm=intel
is understood: a name in quotes, which is a symbol whatever it spells
and may have a relocation operator on it
.Pq Ql call \&"free\&"@PLT ;
.Ql OFFSET FLAT:sym
for the address of a symbol; a symbol before the brackets of its
address
.Pq Ql .LC0[rip] ;
and brackets around what a jump goes through.
A
.Sq d
after the name of an instruction of general registers is the
.Sq l
of AT&T syntax:
.Ql addd [eax], 5 .
A size that the instruction has not is refused:
.Ql paddd xmm1, BYTE PTR [eax] .
.Cm .code16 , .code32
and
.Cm .code64
set the mode what follows is encoded for, whatever the options said
and whatever the object is.
.Cm .code16gcc
is 16-bit code in which a call, a return and the instructions of the
stack are of 32 bits where nothing says otherwise, as a compiler for
32-bit code writes them.
With no
.Fl march
every instruction set is assembled.
.Fl march=x86-64-v1 , v2
and
.Cm v3
refuse what a later level adds, and say which: AVX, AVX2, FMA and BMI
want
.Cm x86-64-v3 ,
and AVX-512 \(em a
.Li %zmm
register, one past the fifteenth, a mask, a broadcast or a way of
rounding \(em wants
.Cm x86-64-v4 .
The instruction sets are those GNU as 2.47 has, but for APX: among
them AVX512-FP16, AVX10.2, AMX, CMPccXADD, Key Locker, MPX, and of
AMD's XOP, TBM, LWP and 3DNow!.
An instruction that both AVX and AVX-512 can say is AVX's unless
.Li {evex}
is written before it or its operands are AVX-512's;
.Li {vex}
and
.Li {vex3}
ask for the other.
.It ARM and AArch64
Run as
.Nm arm-as
or
.Nm aarch64-as ,
or with
.Fl -target
naming one,
.Nm
reads that machine's source and writes that machine's object: ELF32 for
ARM, with its addends in the instructions, and ELF64 for AArch64, with
them in the relocations.
.Pp
For ARM: the data-processing instructions, the multiplies and divides,
loads and stores of one register and of several, branches,
.Li svc ,
.Li mrs ,
.Li msr ,
.Li mrc
and
.Li mcr ,
the hints, and of VFP and NEON the arithmetic, comparisons,
conversions, moves, loads and stores.
A mnemonic's
.Li s
and its condition may be in either order.
After
.Li .thumb
the same in Thumb, in 16 bits where 16 can say what is written and
.Li .w
does not ask for 32, with the flags set by the mnemonic and by whether
an
.Li it
block holds the instruction.
A Thumb branch is 32 bits unless
.Li .n
is written, and is not shortened by how near its label is.
For AArch64: data processing with a number and with a register,
bit fields, conditional selection, loads and stores of one register and
of two, branches, the system instructions and registers, and of the
floats and vectors the arithmetic, comparisons, conversions, moves of
elements and loads and stores.
.Pp
.Li "ldr reg, =value"
is a move where one instruction can move the value and otherwise a load
from a literal pool, which is put where
.Li .ltorg
or
.Li .pool
is and, for what none of those took, at the end of its section.
.Li .word
is four bytes,
.Li .inst
an instruction's
.Po
in Thumb a half word or two, by the number or by
.Li .inst.n
and
.Li .inst.w
.Pc ,
and
.Li .align
a power of two.
.Li .arm ,
.Li .thumb
and
.Li .code
choose ARM's instruction set, and
.Li .thumb_func ,
or
.Li .type
calling a label of Thumb code a function, sets the low bit of the
symbol.
.Li .syntax ,
.Li .arch ,
.Li .arch_extension ,
.Li .object_arch ,
.Li .cpu
and
.Li .fpu
are checked and change nothing: every instruction
.Nm
has is allowed.
.Li .eabi_attribute
makes
.Li .ARM.attributes .
The directives of ARM's unwinding tables \(em
.Li .fnstart , .fnend , .cantunwind , .save , .vsave , .pad , .setfp ,
.Li .movsp , .personality , .personalityindex , .handlerdata
and
.Li .unwind_raw
\(em are refused, no table being made.
A comment is from
.Li @
in ARM, from
.Li //
in both, and from a
.Li #
that begins its line.
.Pp
.Fl mcpu ,
.Fl mfpu ,
.Fl mfloat-abi
and
.Fl march
are taken and change nothing;
.Fl mthumb
begins the source as Thumb.
The mapping symbols
.Pq Li $a , $t , $d
are not written, so a disassembler must be told which instruction set
a Thumb object holds.
.El
.Pp
See
.Pa docs/specs/as_arch_reference.md
for the per-architecture instruction-family appendix.
.Sh NOTE.GNU.PROPERTY
.Nm
writes no
.Pa .note.gnu.property
of its own, at any
.Fl march :
an object does not say which x86-64 level its instructions need.
.Po
This page said that it did, with
.Dv GNU_PROPERTY_X86_ISA_1_NEEDED ;
it never has.
GNU
.Nm
writes none by itself either.
.Pc
A source may write the section itself.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width AS_BACKEND
.It Ev AS_CPP
Override preprocessor executable for
.Pa .S
inputs.
.It Ev AS_DUMP_EXPANDED
If set, the source as it stands after macros, repeats and conditionals
are expanded is written to standard error before it is assembled.
A line that begins
.Ql #@
says which line of which file the next one came from.
The expansion is done in memory: no intermediate file is written.
.It Ev AS_ERROR_CODES
If non-empty,
.Nm
prefixes driver-originated diagnostics with stable internal error IDs.
.It Ev AS_MAX_INPUT_BYTES
Default for
.Fl -max-input-bytes .
.It Ev AS_MAX_LINE_BYTES
Default for
.Fl -max-line-bytes .
.It Ev AS_NO_STMT_CODE
If set, the bytes of an instruction are worked out each time a pass
wants them, and not kept from the first.
The object is the same either way; the variable is for the test that
holds it so.
.It Ev AS_CHECK_FAR_FORMS
If set, each jump or call whose bytes are taken from those kept for
its mnemonic is also worked out afresh, and the assembler aborts if the
two differ.
For testing; an assembly is slower with it and the object is the same.
.It Ev AS_MAX_TOKEN_LENGTH
Default for
.Fl -max-token-length .
.It Ev AS_MAX_MACRO_DEPTH
Default for
.Fl -max-macro-depth .
.It Ev AS_MAX_INCLUDE_DEPTH
Default for
.Fl -max-include-depth .
.It Ev AS_DEBUG_RELOC_TRACE
If non-empty, each instruction is written to standard error as its
relocations are made, with where it stands and how long it is.
For whoever is debugging the assembler.
.It Ev AS_DEBUG_EMIT_PHASE
If set, the object writer says on standard error as it begins each of
its passes.
For whoever is debugging the assembler.
.It Ev SUBSTRATE_TC_DEPTH , SUBSTRATE_TC_MAX_DEPTH , SUBSTRATE_TC_TRACE
The compiler driver, the assembler and the linker count in
.Ev SUBSTRATE_TC_DEPTH
how deep one has run another, and name themselves in
.Ev SUBSTRATE_TC_TRACE ;
.Nm
adds one to the count and, where it is already
.Ev SUBSTRATE_TC_MAX_DEPTH
.Pq 64 if that is unset, and never less than 4 ,
fails with the trace, so that tools that run each other without end are
stopped.
.Nm
also sets
.Ev SUBSTRATE_AS_ACTIVE
for what it runs.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "docs/specs/as_arch_reference.md"
.It Pa docs/specs/as_spec.md
Assembler formal specification.
.It Pa docs/specs/as_rollout.md
Rollout and release gating matrix.
.It Pa docs/specs/as_arch_reference.md
Per-architecture instruction-family appendix.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "123"
.It 0
Successful assembly and validation.
.It 1
Backend execution, validation, or internal frontend failure; an option
there is not.
.It 2
Usage/argument error.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
What is wrong with a line of the source is written to standard error as
.Pp
.Dl file:line: Error: text
.Pp
and a warning as
.Pp
.Dl file:line: Warning: text
.Pp
where the file and line are those the line was written at, whatever
macros, repeats, conditionals and included files it came through; the
standard input is
.Ql {standard input} .
For a line of a macro's expansion the line given is the one the macro
was called on, and the message ends with which macro it was:
.Pp
.Dl file:line: Error: text (in the expansion of macro 'name')
.Pp
Every statement in error is reported, not the first alone.
What is wrong with the run itself, such as an option that is not one,
begins with the program's name and the word
.Ql error .
.Pp
When an assembly fails no object is written, and a file of the output's
name left by an earlier run is removed.
Ended by
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGHUP ,
the assembler removes what it had begun to write and dies of the signal.
